  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A coastal low pressure system will bring heavy \nrain, strong wind gusts, and coastal flooding Thanksgiving Day.\n\nNo significant model trends to report this cycle with respect to the \ncoastal storm to impact the Mid-Atlantic coast on Thanksgiving Day. \nModels seem to have honed in on the track just east of I-95 \nwith only subtle speed differences. \n\nLatest EPS output now shows a higher prob of 2\" of rain south of I-\n66 and US-50 that it showed yesterday. Winds showed a slight \nincrease in magnitude with gusts of 40-55 mph likely across much of \nthe area and gusts as high as 70 mph along the Chesapeake Bay and \nhigher elevations due to an intense 925 mb easterly jet of 70kt. \nMinor coastal flooding seems like a safe bet with the potential for \nmoderate at Anne Arundel and St. Marys Counties due the strong \nonshore flow combined with high astronomical tides associated with a \nFull Moon today. \n\nThe storm system departs the local area late Thu evening with steady \nrain ending, with showers turning into snow showers across the \nmountains and broken stratocumulus and gusty WSW winds across the \nrest of the area.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Cool temperatures through mid-week, with a\ngradual warming trend behind the coastal low this weekend.\n\nOutside of the high-impact coastal system, mainly quiet\nconditions are expected through mid-week, and again into the\nlast weekend of November. \n\nFor the current conditions, the earlier cold front now stretches\nfrom Nova Scotia down to the coastal Carolinas. Any anafrontal\nshowers have largely shifted just east of the Delmarva\nPeninsula. The GOES-19 multi-layer water vapor channel shows\nplenty of dry air moving in from the Great Lakes. Some recent\naircraft soundings show that dry air, particularly in the mid-\nlevels of the troposphere. A decent amount of mixing is still\noccurring in the boundary layer which is supported by periodic\ngusts up to 15 to 20 mph around the area. This all eventually\nsets the stages for a dry and brisk day ahead.\n\nThe passage of the upper trough will allow for continued\nnortherly cold advection today. Forecast highs reach the upper\n40s to low 50s, with mainly 30s to low 40s in the mountains.\nThis is accompanied by abundant sunshine, but with northerly\ngusts up to 20 mph. A strong area of Canadian high pressure is\nset to move from the Great Lakes toward New England tonight. By\ntonight into Wednesday morning, the forecast shows surface\npressures as high as 1041 mb over upstate New York. With the\nridge sagging southward along the spine of the Appalachians, a\ncold night looms ahead. Tonight's lows will largely range from\nthe upper teens to 20s. Areas along and east of I-95 should stay\ncloser to the freezing mark. Heading into Wednesday, highs\nremain fairly close to today, but this comes with increasing\nclouds and a shift to northeasterly flow. Such winds pick up in\nstrength into the night as gusty showers move in from the south.\nOvernight gusts around 25 to 35 mph are expected, with up to 45\nmph across the mountains. For more information on the\nThanksgiving forecast, please see Key Message 1 (above).\n\nThe period spanning Friday and into Saturday sees the coastal\nlow pushing north of the Mason-Dixon Line into New England.\nTight gradients persist which will keep brisk winds in the\nforecast. The net winds are mainly out of the west to southwest\nwhich is not the most favorable for upslope activity along/west\nof the Alleghenies. However, still expect some light\naccumulations possible throughout Friday into Saturday morning.\nFor the weekend into early next week, global ensembles show a\nslow but steady warm up. Overall precipitation chances appear\nmeager to finish out the month.",
